The accompanying [[music video]]s for "You're Still the One" was directed by David Hogan.{{sfn|Brown|2000|p=157}} Twain explained to Hogan her initial vision of light as the main effect, and Hogan subsequently sent her photography books to choose lighting imagery and effects.<ref name="VideoBio">{{cite AV media|date=August 8, 1998|title=Shania's Video Bio|type=Television production|publisher=CMT}}</ref> Twain picked a dappled "dramatic" lighting, which Hogan "duplicated [...] perfectly".{{sfn|Brown|2000|p=157}}<ref name="VideoBio" /> The music video for "You're Still the One" was shot in [[black-and-white]] in [[Malibu, California]].{{sfn|Eggar|2001|p=257}}<ref>{{cite magazine|volume=110|issue=9|page=77|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Eg8EAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA77|issn=0006-2510|date=February 28, 1998|title=Production notes|magazine=Billboard}}</ref> Twain said the majority of the video was supposed to be shot on the beach.<ref name="VideoBio" /> However, due to unfavorable weather conditions, several scenes were filmed in a beach house.<ref name="VideoBio" /> The video features interspersed scenes of Twain on a beach and male model John Devoe in a beach house.{{sfn|Brown|2000|p=157}}<ref name="BootVideo">{{cite web|url=https://theboot.com/top-shania-twain-music-videos/|title=Top 10 Shania Twain Music Videos|website=The Boot|first=Christina|last=Vinson|date=September 23, 2022|access-date=June 16, 2023|archive-date=January 1, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230101114802/http://theboot.com/top-shania-twain-music-videos/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Breithaupt">{{cite news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/119076166/national-post/|title=Twain explained|last=Breithaupt|first=Jeff|date=March 20, 2000|work=[[National Post]]|pages=D1|access-date=February 19, 2023|via=[[Newspapers.com]]}}</ref> Close-up shots of Twain show her singing on a beach at night, under the full moon.{{sfn|Brown|2000|p=157}}<ref name="Breithaupt" /> She is wearing a silk robe and has wet hair.<ref name="BootVideo" /><ref name="Johnson">{{Cite news|last=Johnson|first=Brian D.|author-link=Brian D. Johnson|date=March 23, 1998|title=Shania Revealed|magazine=[[Maclean's]]|url=https://archive.macleans.ca/article/1998/3/23/shania-revealed|page=50|access-date=September 19, 2025|archive-date=September 2, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210902060202/https://archive.macleans.ca/article/1998/3/23/shania-revealed|url-status=dead}}</ref> Devoe is seen lounging in a bathtub and watching Twain on TV.<ref name="BootVideo" />{{sfn|Mandrell|2014|p=1019}} Towards the end of the video, after he gets out of the tub, he takes off his towel and joins Twain in bed.<ref name="Breithaupt" /><ref name="Johnson" />{{sfn|Mandrell|2014|p=1019}}

Brown complimented the "spectacular" contrast between outdoor and indoor scenes.{{sfn|Brown|2000|p=157}} He found it reminiscent of black-and-white romance films of the 1940s.{{sfn|Brown|2000|p=157}} Writing for [[NPR]], [[Ann Powers]] described the music video as a "mix of beachy sensuality and technologically assisted intimacy" which she compared to [[Janet Jackson]]'s "[[Love Will Never Do (Without You)]]" (1990) and [[Jennifer Lopez]]'s "[[If You Had My Love]]" (1999).<ref name="NPR">{{cite web|url=https://www.npr.org/sections/therecord/2011/05/08/136060728/shania-twain-a-survivor-who-remade-the-good-old-girl|title=Shania Twain: a survivor who remade the good old girl|publisher=[[NPR]]|first=Ann|last=Powers|author-link=Ann Powers|date=May 6, 2011|access-date=June 16, 2023|archive-date=January 8, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200108122010/https://www.npr.org/sections/therecord/2011/05/08/136060728/shania-twain-a-survivor-who-remade-the-good-old-girl|url-status=live}}</ref> Scenes from the music video were deemed sexual and sparked controversy among conservative country music critics.{{sfn|McCall|1999|p=135}} Twain responded to these remarks as "totally ridiculous".{{sfn|McCall|1999|p=135}} She commented, "There is nothing revealing about it. I'm wrapped up to the gills. The video is sensual and has a surreal feel about it, but there is nothing sexual about it. When you start kissing and touching, it's sexual. But sensual? That's fine, in my opinion — it's a very romantic song".{{sfn|McCall|1999|p=136}} "You're Still the One" was the first music video by a country artist to be added to the U.S. [[MTV]] playlist.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-vancouver-sun/119013442/|title=Naysers' doubts are put to rest by Twain concert debut|last=Flynn|first=Andrew|date=June 1, 1998|work=[[Vancouver Sun]]|page=C3|access-date=February 18, 2023|via=[[Newspapers.com]]}}</ref> It won Best Country Clip at the 1998 ''Billboard'' Music Video Awards and was nominated for [[MTV Video Music Award for Best Female Video|Best Female Video]] at the [[1998 MTV Video Music Awards]].<ref>{{cite magazine|volume=110|issue=48|page=60|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=MAoEAAAAMBAJ&pg=RA1-PA60|issn=0006-2510|date=November 28, 1998|title=''Billboard'' Music Video confab celebrates 20th birthday|magazine=Billboard}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine|volume=110|issue=30|page=92|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=9gkEAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA92|issn=0006-2510|date=July 25, 1998|last=Hay|first=Carla|title=Madonna tops MTV noms|magazine=Billboard}}</ref> This made Twain the first female country artist to be nominated for an MTV Video Music Award.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/star-tribune/119015592/|title=Twain comes on over – live|last=Bream|first=Jon|date=July 24, 1998|work=[[Star Tribune]]|pages=E5|access-date=February 18, 2023|via=[[Newspapers.com]]}}</ref>

